WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.475
Picture a restaurant with a strange manager.

2
00:00:05.475 --> 00:00:08.187
For every order, he hires a brand-new cook.

3
00:00:08.187 --> 00:00:12.917
The cook signs a contract, puts on an apron, cooks one dish, and goes home.

4
00:00:13.133 --> 00:00:16.418
For a slow roast, all that paperwork hardly matters.

5
00:00:16.418 --> 00:00:20.144
For a side salad, the contract takes longer than the salad.

6
00:00:20.367 --> 00:00:27.575
Plenty of Java code works like this manager: for each task, a piece of work to run, it starts a new thread.

7
00:00:27.575 --> 00:00:28.518
It's one line.

8
00:00:28.518 --> 00:00:31.684
So here's a question we're a little shy to ask.

9
00:00:31.684 --> 00:00:33.705
How expensive can one line be?

10
00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:37.765
Let's map it first.

11
00:00:37.765 --> 00:00:43.024
A cook is a thread, one path of work running beside others in the same program.

12
00:00:43.024 --> 00:00:44.288
An order is a task.

13
00:00:44.288 --> 00:00:47.151
And hiring a cook is starting a new thread.

14
00:00:47.367 --> 00:00:53.188
An ordinary Java thread is a platform thread, which wraps one thread of the operating system.

15
00:00:53.188 --> 00:00:57.757
Starting one is a trip into the kernel, the core of the operating system.

16
00:00:57.757 --> 00:01:03.202
The new thread gets its own stack, the memory for its method calls, with 1 MB reserved.

17
00:01:03.202 --> 00:01:07.959
And it gets a place in the scheduler, which picks the thread that runs next.

18
00:01:08.167 --> 00:01:13.896
That's the contract and the apron, and they're torn down and paid again for every dish.

19
00:01:13.896 --> 00:01:16.793
The round trip takes about 130 microseconds.

20
00:01:16.793 --> 00:01:21.007
Our tasks are side salads, a few nanoseconds of arithmetic each.

21
00:01:21.007 --> 00:01:24.300
So each task pays thousands of times its own cost.

22
00:01:24.533 --> 00:01:27.217
What we want is the way real kitchens work.

23
00:01:27.217 --> 00:01:30.712
They hire a team of cooks once, say four, and keep them.

24
00:01:30.933 --> 00:01:33.823
Every order goes on a ticket, clipped to a rail.

25
00:01:33.823 --> 00:01:38.819
A cook with free hands takes the next ticket, cooks it, and comes back for another.

26
00:01:39.033 --> 00:01:40.305
That's a thread pool.

27
00:01:40.305 --> 00:01:45.331
Each of its threads runs one loop: take a task from a queue, run it, take the next.

28
00:01:45.331 --> 00:01:50.841
The cooks are the threads, the rail is the queue, and the number of cooks is the pool size.

29
00:01:50.841 --> 00:01:53.929
It also caps how many tasks run at the same moment.

30
00:01:54.133 --> 00:01:56.183
So let's put a price on it.

31
00:01:56.183 --> 00:02:04.153
The lesson's demo runs 5,000 tiny tasks on 4 cores, first with a new thread each, then on a pool of four.

32
00:02:04.153 --> 00:02:06.734
How many times faster is the pool?

33
00:02:06.734 --> 00:02:07.189
Twice?

34
00:02:07.189 --> 00:02:07.948
Ten times?

35
00:02:10.733 --> 00:02:13.832
Here's one run, on a busy machine.

36
00:02:13.832 --> 00:02:17.295
A new thread each: 2,635 milliseconds.

37
00:02:17.295 --> 00:02:18.480
The pool: 44.

38
00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:25.224
Over two rounds, that's 60 to 150 times faster, for exactly the same work.

39
00:02:25.433 --> 00:02:29.088
A quiet machine is faster at both, and the gap stays huge.

40
00:02:29.088 --> 00:02:32.807
So yes, the contracts really did cost more than the salads.

41
00:02:33.033 --> 00:02:37.201
There's a second cost, and it hides until the busiest hour.

42
00:02:37.201 --> 00:02:39.532
A thread per task has no ceiling.

43
00:02:39.532 --> 00:02:43.912
If 3,000 orders arrive at once, the manager hires 3,000 cooks.

44
00:02:44.133 --> 00:02:52.618
Something runs out first: the operating system's limit on threads, memory for their stacks, or time spent switching between them.

45
00:02:52.618 --> 00:02:58.867
In Java, the first sign is often an OutOfMemoryError that says: unable to create native thread.

46
00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:07.858
With a pool, those 3,000 orders become 3,000 tickets on the rail, not 3,000 cooks squeezed into a kitchen with four stoves.

47
00:03:07.858 --> 00:03:11.490
So a thread per tiny task costs more than the task.

48
00:03:11.490 --> 00:03:14.053
Create threads once, and reuse them.

49
00:03:14.267 --> 00:03:15.937
Every kitchen also closes.

50
00:03:15.937 --> 00:03:21.011
The gentle way: lock the front door, but cook every ticket already on the rail.

51
00:03:21.011 --> 00:03:25.636
The hard way: hand the waiting tickets back, and tell the cooks to stop.

52
00:03:25.867 --> 00:03:28.515
Java's gentle close is called shutdown.

53
00:03:28.515 --> 00:03:37.207
The lesson's demo gives two cooks six orders of 300 milliseconds each, then calls shutdown, and prints the time when it returns.

54
00:03:37.207 --> 00:03:38.089
When is that?

55
00:03:40.867 --> 00:03:44.411
At 115 milliseconds, before a single order is cooked.

56
00:03:44.411 --> 00:03:48.891
Then a seventh order is turned away, because the kitchen is closed.

57
00:03:48.891 --> 00:03:54.241
And the six on the rail still get cooked, two at a time, 300 milliseconds apart.

58
00:03:54.467 --> 00:03:57.136
So shutdown locks the door and returns.

59
00:03:57.136 --> 00:03:58.574
It waits for nothing.

60
00:03:58.574 --> 00:04:02.749
Waiting is a separate call, awaitTermination, with a timeout.

61
00:04:02.749 --> 00:04:07.677
Here it returned true at 1,007 milliseconds, right after the last order.

62
00:04:07.900 --> 00:04:09.776
The hard close is shutdownNow.

63
00:04:09.776 --> 00:04:13.029
In a second run, it's called after 100 milliseconds.

64
00:04:13.029 --> 00:04:18.032
It hands back the four tickets that never started, and interrupts the two cooks.

65
00:04:18.233 --> 00:04:20.857
An interrupt is a polite request to stop.

66
00:04:20.857 --> 00:04:26.232
Orders 1 and 2 were waiting out their 300 milliseconds, so they woke up and stopped.

67
00:04:26.232 --> 00:04:28.280
Here the kitchen picture breaks.

68
00:04:28.280 --> 00:04:30.392
A real cook hears you shout stop.

69
00:04:30.392 --> 00:04:34.935
A Java task hears it only if it checks, or waits in a method that does.

70
00:04:34.935 --> 00:04:37.431
A loop that never checks keeps cooking.

71
00:04:37.633 --> 00:04:39.657
And if you never close the pool?

72
00:04:39.657 --> 00:04:42.376
Then you meet the most loyal cooks in town.

73
00:04:42.376 --> 00:04:47.625
Its threads aren't daemon threads, the background helpers the JVM doesn't wait for.

74
00:04:47.625 --> 00:04:53.191
So after main returns, they wait by the empty rail forever, and the program never exits.

75
00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:58.432
In code, since JDK 19, put the pool in try-with-resources, or Kotlin's use.

76
00:04:58.432 --> 00:05:02.458
At the end, close calls shutdown, then waits for every task.

77
00:05:02.458 --> 00:05:09.569
With a deadline, call shutdown, then awaitTermination with a timeout, then shutdownNow for the stragglers.

78
00:05:09.800 --> 00:05:11.947
So, how expensive can one line be?

79
00:05:11.947 --> 00:05:16.493
A new thread per tiny task costs more than the task, and has no ceiling.

80
00:05:16.493 --> 00:05:23.943
Hire the cooks once, give them one rail, and at closing time, lock the door, and then wait for the last cook to leave.

81
00:05:24.167 --> 00:05:25.022
Quick check.

82
00:05:25.022 --> 00:05:28.941
A pool of 4 has 10 tasks waiting, each one second long.

83
00:05:28.941 --> 00:05:31.650
main calls shutdown, then prints done.

84
00:05:31.650 --> 00:05:33.217
When does done appear?

85
00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:36.926
Right away.

86
00:05:36.926 --> 00:05:42.144
shutdown only locks the door, and the tasks keep running after done is printed.
